LS(1)                       User Commands

NAME
    ls - list directory contents

SYNOPSIS
    ls [-1ClhatrSdF] [file...]

DESCRIPTION
    Lists names in columns on a terminal, one per line in a pipe. -1 forces
    one name per line; -C forces columns. -l shows permissions, link count,
    size and modification date; -h uses KiB-based units. -a includes hidden
    names. -t sorts by modification time, -S by size, -r reverses order.
    -d lists directories themselves, -F adds / for directories, @ for
    symbolic links, * for executables and | for pipes. Names are sorted
    alphabetically otherwise. Colours distinguish directories, symbolic
    links, executable files and devices when stdout is a terminal, TERM
    is set and NO_COLOR is absent.

    The entries of a directory are shown as they are, so a symbolic link
    appears as a link: -l marks it with l and prints "name -> target",
    with the length of the target as its size. A link named as an
    operand is followed, and a link to a directory lists that directory,
    unless -l, -d or -F is given, in which case the link itself is
    shown.

SEE ALSO
    sh(1), man(1), index(7)
